    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, chronic (F43.12)</content>. PCL-5 46, from 56: improving, not yet a meaningful change. He chose sertraline, which the VA/DoD guideline recommends, at a low starting dose for his age. Prazosin, which the guideline suggests for nightmares, is not started now because of orthostatic falls risk after his fall; it will be reconsidered with standing blood pressure checks if nightmares persist after therapy and sertraline. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm. [S]<list><item>Sertraline 25 mg each morning.</item><item>Sodium within two to four weeks, then the dose raised to 50 mg if tolerated.</item><item>Continue cognitive processing therapy.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, moderate (F10.20)</content>. Drinking much less on naltrexone; liver tests falling. [S]<list><item>Continue naltrexone and thiamine.</item><item>PEth and liver tests at the next visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PTSD symptom severity (PCL-5)</td><td>Baseline, then every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>46, from 56</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Trauma-focused psychotherapy (cognitive processing therapy), sessions attended</td><td>Weekly for twelve sessions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Four of twelve sessions attended</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depressive symptoms (PHQ-9)</td><td>Baseline, then monthly during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>10, from 11; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; 988 card carried; sertraline dispensed four weeks at a time</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Drinking since the last visit, against his own goal</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two evenings a week, two drinks each</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (AST, ALT, GGT) on naltrexone</td><td>Before starting, at four to six weeks, then every three to six months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>AST 41, ALT 31, GGT 84 U/L, four weeks on naltrexone</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PEth, a blood marker of drinking over the past two to four weeks</td><td>At baseline and every one to three months during treatment, with consent</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Due at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naltrexone safety: opioid use, nausea, wallet card</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No opioid use; no nausea</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI safety: sodium, bleeding, falls and side effects</td><td>Sodium within the first month in older adults; review at each dose change</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Sertraline 25 mg started today; sodium due in two to four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prazosin for nightmares</td><td>When nightmares persist despite first-line treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not started now: orthostatic falls risk in a man of 77 who fell recently; reconsider after therapy and sertraline</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Falls and gait (Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure</td><td>At intake and every visit after a fall</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Timed Up and Go 12 seconds; standing pressure 130/76; no falls</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Traditional healing alongside clinic care, at his choice</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>His choice; nothing asked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Sleep most nights with nightmares no more than once a week. In progress; target 1906-10-28 [S]</item><item>Lower PTSD symptoms by a meaningful amount (PCL-5 down at least 10 to 20 points). In progress; target 1906-10-28 [S]</item><item>Drink no more than one drink on any day, and no drinking before riding. In progress; target 1906-10-28 [S]</item><item>No further falls. In progress; target 1906-10-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) in Adults (2017); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Pharmacological Treatment of Patients With Alcohol Use Disorder (2018); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Special General Memorandum 94-08: Statement of Policy for the Traditional Cultural Advocacy Program (1994); American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); Falls Prevention in Community-Dwelling Older Adults: Interventions (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Sodium in two to four weeks. Eleven-week review with PEth and liver tests. Therapy weekly. Call the clinic or 988 at any hour. Next scheduled visit 1906-09-23: eleven-week review: therapy progress, sertraline and sodium, drinking and falls. [S]</paragraph></text>

